WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.020
Good to have you on SpaceX Daily, episode 48. It's July 29th, 2026, and I'm Patrick in Vancouver. Here's what's happening at SpaceX today. Ship 40's continued float after Flight 13 gives SpaceX its first long duration data set on an intact upper stage thermal protection system in seawater. SpaceX confirmed the ship upper stage completed its landing burn and splashdown intact on Flight 13.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:57.020
imagery gathered by the recovery team shows the vehicle still on the surface with no visible structural breach. Elon Musk stated a dedicated ship is in route to retrieve the stage for post flight inspection. The extended float period supplies the first extended seawater exposure data set on the current heat shield tile and skirt configuration. Recovery operations will focus on preserving the as flown condition of the tile array and skirt ring for laboratory analysis.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:15.620
The data will quantify salt ingress, residual bond strength, and any hydrodynamic deformation accumulated over multiple days. This follows the Flight 13 performance details we discussed yesterday and supplies the next concrete data point on upper stage reuse durability.

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:38.740
Flight 13 placed the first star link V3 satellites into orbit during the ship upper stages orbital phase. These satellites carry upgraded laser link terminals and higher power phased array antennas compared with prior generations. The deployment marks the first on orbit test of the V3 bus design under starship mass and volume margins.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:54.280
Early telemetry will be compared against Falcon launched V2 mini units to quantify the performance delta. The V3 units also incorporate revised power and thermal subsystems sized for the increased aperture and processing load.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:02:01.860
Ground stations are already receiving beacon signals that confirm initial attitude control and solar array deployment.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:11.580
A spent Falcon 9 second stage is projected to impact the lunar surface in the coming days after a prior mission left it in lunar transfer trajectory.

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:20.760
The stage will strike the moon at several kilometers per second. Impact location and timing are being refined with updated orbital elements.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:29.420
The event supplies an unplanned high speed kinetic test of lunar regular interaction at energies relevant to future lander designs.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:37.720
Observers expect the impact flash and ejecta plume to be captured by earth based telescopes and lunar orbiters already in place.

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:45.180
The data will help calibrate models of crater formation and seismic coupling used for Artemis landing site assessments.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:54.780
The Federal Aviation Administration proposes waving certain environmental reviews to shorten launch license timelines for SpaceX and Blue Origin.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:03:03.540
The draft rule would allow operators to reference prior environmental assessments rather than repeat full studies for each incremental change.

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:10.020
SpaceX's starbase and Cape Canaveral sites would see the largest reduction in paperwork lead time.

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:15.180
The change directly affects cadence targets for both Falcon and Starship vehicles.

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:23.880
Agency staff noted that the proposal still requires public comment and coordination with other federal offices before final adoption.

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:31.700
If implemented, the streamlined process could compress the interval between license applications and approvals by several months.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:37.980
Falcon 9 stands ready for the NRL 25 National Security Mission at Vandenberg.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:43.640
The vehicle is stacked and undergoing final checks at Vandenberg for the classified payload.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:49.780
The mission continues the pattern of dedicated national security flights on reused first stages.

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:54.200
No public timeline has been released beyond the standing net window.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:04:02.240
The payload customer is the National Reconnaissance Office, consistent with prior NRO missions flown on Falcon 9.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:08.920
Pre-launch processing includes standard health checks on the Merlin engines and stage separation systems.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:16.600
SpaceX and Italy are discussing a $1.6 billion starlink contract for maritime and aviation coverage.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:22.920
The proposed multi-year agreement would expand coverage over the Mediterranean and surrounding regions.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:29.740
Negotiations center on capacity guarantees and ground segment integration with Italian infrastructure.

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:38.880
No final terms have been announced. The deal would add dedicated gateways and user terminal certification pathways for Italian operators.

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:45.320
Italian defense and civil aviation authorities are participating in the technical discussions.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:52.640
AST space mobile booked three additional Bluebird satellites on Falcon 9 for an August 5 launch.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:58.640
The satellites will test direct to cell connectivity and partnership with existing mobile carriers.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:05.060
This marks the second dedicated Falcon 9 mission for the constellation after an earlier ride share.

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:11.980
The August 5 window is the first of three planned launches that will populate the initial operational plane.

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:19.580
Each Bluebird carries a large phased array antenna designed for direct communication with unmodified handsets.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:26.560
24 additional starlink satellites reached orbit on a Falcon 9 launch the day after flight 13.

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:31.780
The mission used a previously flown first stage that completed its 20th flight.

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:38.360
Deployment occurred into a 550 kilometers circular orbit for the broadband constellation.

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:46.620
The booster was recovered on the drone ship after separation, continuing the high reuse cadence established on the Falcon fleet.

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:53.520
All 24 satellites reported nominal deployment and initial orbit raising burns within the first hour.

00:05:54.520 --> 00:06:03.080
Observers on the ground are circulating two new videos of ship 40's landing burn that show the visible plume interaction with the ocean surface at ignition.

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:12.120
Indian Ocean Maritime Trackers report the recovery vessels approximate heading an estimated arrival window based on public AI's data.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:19.940
Several users posted annotated imagery comparing the current tile pattern on ship 40 with earlier test articles.

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:32.080
One thing worth watching is that NASA heat shield specialists have stated that starships current tiled thermal protection approach may prove a developmental dead end for repeated lunar return entries.

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:41.540
The assessment centers on tile attachment durability under higher energy trajectories and the difficulty of inspecting or repairing the array between flights.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:48.880
Resolution would require either a redesigned attachment system or a shift to an ableative or metallic alternative.

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:56.440
The critique draws on decades of shuttle and Orion experience with reusable versus single use thermal protection.

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:11.220
On the AI front, GROC 4.6 and 4.7 releases are scheduled in quick succession with early specifications pointing to expanded context length and improved tool use capabilities inside cursor.

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:19.640
The paired releases continue XAI's pattern of rapid iteration on the same training cluster used for prior GROC versions.

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:28.560
Cursor introduced an India-only subscription tier priced at 649 rupees per month, aimed at the country's large developer base.

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:37.920
The tier is positioned as an entry point for students and smaller teams while preserving access to the same model routing features available in higher tiers.

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:47.920
Cursor customers are pushing back on proposed price increases during contract renewals, citing usage patterns that already favor lower cost model routing.

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:57.940
Enterprise teams report that the majority of their daily edits are now handled by the cheaper models without quality regression, reducing the effective cost per token.

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:04.840
Cursor has patched a high severity get vulnerability that remained open for seven months before remediation.

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:13.400
The delay drew internal review of the project's security response process and prompted updated triage timelines for future reports.

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:26.140
From an engineering standpoint, the ship 40 float supplies the first real world data set on how the current tile to skirt interfaces behave when saltwater sits against them for days rather than minutes.

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:38.860
The key unknowns are capillary wicking along the tile edges, galvanic effects at the metallic skirt ring, and whether the adhesive bonds lose sheer strength once sea water reaches the substrate.

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:51.120
Each of those mechanisms scales directly with exposure time, so four days of continuous contact gives a measurable step change over the short duration splash downs seen on earlier flights.

00:08:51.120 --> 00:09:02.760
If salt crystals form inside micro cracks after the water evaporates, the next reentry heat pulse could drive those cracks open faster than the original qualification tests predicted.

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:14.940
The recovery teams decision to prioritize preserving the as flown tile array therefore makes sense, because any post retrieval cleaning would erase exactly the evidence needed to close that loop.

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:24.520
The same data set will also let engineers compare hydrodynamic deformation of the skirt against the structural models used to size the current ring thickness.

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:35.980
A clear result either way feeds straight into the next tile attachment revision or confirms that the present configuration already meets the repeated use margin for lunar return energies.

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:42.740
Before we go watch for the first port side inspection images once the recovery vessel reaches ship 40.

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:46.400
That covers everything worth knowing about SpaceX today.

